"A temple has many ritual instruments, called faqi. The dictionary lists instruments such as the fish, the cymbals, and the bell. The wooden fish has two shapes. One is a long, straight fish that hangs in a corridor, to call people to a meal or to gather them. The other is round, and it sits on a table when chanting. The round one is hollow, and the hollow sound changes with its size, material, and the hollow inside. Chinese Wikipedia says a bigger one has a lower pitch.": "7f2f663345a4",
"A temple has many ritual instruments.": "8cba81378db2",
"How do many voices breathe? A singer cannot hold a note forever, so there must be breaths. If everyone breathes at the same time, there is a small gap in the sound. A technique for choirs, called staggered breathing, has each singer breathe at a different time, so that no breath gaps can be heard. We use it in our model to show the idea. We do not say that any chanting group does it. Chanting is not one sound either: it varies by tradition, with some more monotone and some more melodic.": "4468ff435a68",
"How is the wooden fish used? English Wikipedia says that in Chan, Seon, Zen, and Thien Buddhism, the wooden fish serves to maintain rhythm during chanting. The mallet often has a rubber-coated tip, which gives a muffled but clear sound. A wooden fish often rests on a cushion, to prevent unpleasant knocking sounds. That is why our model uses a short, quickly fading knock, once on every beat.": "4872f434e716",
"In Chinese Buddhism, chanting is called fanbai. The Taiwan dictionary says it means, in China, chanting short verses or songs of praise. Chinese Wikipedia says it gradually took on a specific melody and rhythm to make reciting easier. The melodies are traditionally passed on by ear from one generation to the next, and the chanting book usually does not mark the melody. In this lesson we look only at the sound and the rhythm.": "4f74688cc9be",
"In Chinese Buddhism, chanting is called fanbai. The Taiwan dictionary says it means, in China, chanting short verses or songs of praise. Chinese Wikipedia says it gradually took on a specific melody and rhythm to make reciting easier. The melodies are traditionally passed on by ear from one generation to the next, and the chanting book usually does not mark the melody. In this lesson we look only at the sound and the rhythm. When a group chants, many people sing the same melody at the same time. In music this is called unison. Someone leads. The dictionary says the leader, the weina, starts the chant and leads the group by striking the small bell, the yinqing, in the morning and evening sessions. Those sessions are held every day, and everyone in the temple takes part. A temple has many ritual instruments, called faqi. The dictionary lists instruments such as the fish, the cymbals, and the bell. The wooden fish has two shapes. One is a long, straight fish that hangs in a corridor, to call people to a meal or to gather them. The other is round, and it sits on a table when chanting. The round one is hollow, and the hollow sound changes with its size, material, and the hollow inside. Chinese Wikipedia says a bigger one has a lower pitch. How is the wooden fish used? English Wikipedia says that in Chan, Seon, Zen, and Thien Buddhism, the wooden fish serves to maintain rhythm during chanting. The mallet often has a rubber-coated tip, which gives a muffled but clear sound. A wooden fish often rests on a cushion, to prevent unpleasant knocking sounds. That is why our model uses a short, quickly fading knock, once on every beat. The small bell, the yinqing, is a small bowl-shaped copper bell with a wooden handle, used to get everyone’s attention. A bigger bowl-shaped bell, the qing, is struck during chanting and ceremonies. Be careful with the word qing. It also names an ancient hanging chime of stone or metal. The dictionary lists the two as different instruments that share a character. How do many voices breathe? A singer cannot hold a note forever, so there must be breaths. If everyone breathes at the same time, there is a small gap in the sound. A technique for choirs, called staggered breathing, has each singer breathe at a different time, so that no breath gaps can be heard. We use it in our model to show the idea. We do not say that any chanting group does it. Chanting is not one sound either: it varies by tradition, with some more monotone and some more melodic.": "29bd7842cb9d",
